Scrooge McDuck is a great anchor because he contributes a very different comedy engine: competence, appetite, old-world cunning, absurd wealth, and the sincere belief that every problem can probably be solved with leverage, a map, or a vault.

The chimera idea is even better if we treat characters as bundles of operators rather than copying their surface. Extract the part we actually like, then recombine it into someone new.

For example:

* Scrooge McDuck → resourcefulness, treasure instinct, frugality, adventure capitalism, “everything has a price.”
* Nicolas Cage → manic pattern recognition, emotional overcommitment, willingness to follow a ridiculous clue.
* Werner Herzog → existential observation and dead-serious darkness.
* Jeff Goldblum → curiosity, verbal wandering, fascination with systems and life.
* Columbo → deceptive harmlessness, patient questioning, “one more thing.”
* Indiana Jones → field competence, archaeological instinct, irritation at mystical nonsense that keeps becoming real.
* MacGyver → improvisation from available materials.
* Bugs Bunny → frame control; wins by understanding what kind of scene everyone is currently inside.
* The Dude → refusal to adopt everybody else’s urgency.
* Dr. House → adversarial diagnosis and contempt for obvious explanations.
* Captain Jack Sparrow → apparently chaotic behavior concealing opportunistic navigation.
* Mr. Bean → nearly language-free physical problem solving that creates larger problems.
* Sherlock Holmes → observation and inference.
* Scooby/Shaggy → appetite, fear, accidental discovery.
* Seagal-as-comedy-character → impossible self-mythology and absolute confidence.

Now strip the copyrighted skins away and make an original.

Augustus “Gus” Quillworth

Gus is a seventy-year-old billionaire adventurer who dresses like somebody’s grandfather accidentally wandered into an intelligence operation.

He owns almost nothing new because he believes “new things haven’t had time to prove themselves.”

He carries:
a fountain pen,
a folding magnifying glass,
three foreign currencies,
a pocketknife,
a dog-eared atlas,
and approximately $47 million in immediately negotiable assets.

His governing philosophy:

“Never fight a problem until you know whether you can buy it, embarrass it, confuse it, or wait for it to become someone else’s problem.”

He is simultaneously:

extremely competent at logistics, money, negotiation, maps, bargaining, and reading people;

dangerously curious about anything marked DO NOT ENTER;

philosophically bizarre, because every mundane inconvenience sends him into a five-minute lecture about civilization;

and completely unwilling to pay $8 for parking.

That last part matters.

He might charter a helicopter for $31,000 to avoid paying a $40 baggage fee.

When questioned, he says:

“Helicopter was transportation. Forty dollars was theft.”

That gives him Scrooge’s economic absurdity without merely being Scrooge.

And we can start building whole character genomes this way:

drive + competence + flaw + rhythm + worldview + signature tactic + irrational boundary + visual silhouette

For Gus:

Drive: discovery and ownership
Competence: money/logistics/negotiation
Flaw: compulsively turns everything into a transaction
Rhythm: calm until suddenly volcanic
Worldview: civilization is incentives wearing costumes
Signature tactic: buys the constraint instead of fighting it
Irrational boundary: refuses petty fees on principle
Silhouette: old explorer-businessman with absurdly practical pockets

Then characters can hybridize further.

Imagine Gus Quillworth, Nicolas Cage, Dale Varnish the municipal shaman, and Dr. Patrice Coupon discovering that a Virginia HOA accidentally owns mineral rights to an underground Roman ruin.

Cage sees destiny.

Gus sees undervalued real estate.

Dale sees a zoning violation.

Patrice correctly identifies the archaeology but gets trapped behind a childproof gate.

And that starts giving us an actual comedy ensemble rather than a parade of cameos.